{
  "set1": [
    {
      "domain": 2,
      "task": "2.1",
      "topics": [
        "Amazon SQS",
        "Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling",
        "Amazon CloudWatch"
      ],
      "prompt": "A company runs an order-processing application on Amazon EC2 instances. A web tier receives orders and sends them directly to a fleet of backend worker instances over HTTP. During flash sales, the backend cannot keep up, requests time out, and orders are lost. The company needs to ensure that no orders are lost and that the worker fleet scales in proportion to the amount of pending work. Which solution meets these requirements?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Place a Network Load Balancer in front of the worker fleet and configure an Auto Scaling group that scales on the NLB ActiveFlowCount metric.",
        "B": "Have the web tier send orders to an Amazon SQS queue, and scale the worker Auto Scaling group with target tracking on a custom backlog-per-instance metric.",
        "C": "Have the web tier publish orders to an Amazon SNS topic with an HTTPS subscription for each worker instance, and configure the worker Auto Scaling group to scale on average CPU utilization.",
        "D": "Move the worker fleet to larger EC2 instance types and configure a scheduled scaling action that adds instances before each planned flash sale."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "B"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option B is Correct\n\n* **Durable buffer:** Amazon SQS stores each order durably until a worker successfully processes and deletes it, so traffic spikes are absorbed instead of dropped.\n* **Scaling on actual work:** A backlog-per-instance metric (visible messages divided by running instances) with target tracking is the AWS-recommended way to scale queue consumers in proportion to pending work.\n* **Loose coupling:** The web tier and workers no longer depend on each other's availability or speed, so each tier scales independently.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option A is incorrect:** A load balancer still delivers requests synchronously. If workers are saturated, requests still time out and orders are lost; there is no persistence layer.\n* **Option C is incorrect:** SNS is push-based and does not hold messages for consumers that are busy. HTTPS delivery retries are limited, so orders can still be lost, and CPU does not reflect the amount of pending work.\n* **Option D is incorrect:** Vertical scaling and scheduled actions do not handle unplanned spikes, and there is still no buffer to prevent lost orders.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **\"Orders must not be lost\" + spiky load:** Put an SQS queue between producer and consumer to decouple the tiers.\n2. **Scaling queue consumers:** Scale on backlog per instance (ApproximateNumberOfMessagesVisible / InService instances) rather than CPU."
    },
    {
      "domain": 2,
      "task": "2.1",
      "topics": [
        "Amazon SNS",
        "Amazon SQS"
      ],
      "prompt": "A retail company has an inventory service that emits an event every time stock levels change. Three downstream microservices must each receive a copy of every relevant event: the reorder service only needs events where the quantity drops below a threshold, the analytics service needs all events, and the pricing service only needs events for the electronics category. Each consumer must be able to process events at its own pace and be taken offline for maintenance without losing events. Which solution meets these requirements with the LEAST development effort?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Publish all events to a single Amazon SQS standard queue that the three microservices poll concurrently, and have each microservice discard any messages that it does not need to process.",
        "B": "Write events to an Amazon DynamoDB table and have each microservice scan the table on a schedule for new items that match its criteria.",
        "C": "Have the inventory service call each microservice's REST API directly and retry failed calls with exponential backoff.",
        "D": "Publish events to an Amazon SNS topic. Subscribe a separate Amazon SQS queue for each microservice, and apply an SNS subscription filter policy to the reorder and pricing subscriptions."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "D"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option D is Correct\n\n* **Fan-out (pub/sub):** SNS delivers a copy of every published message to every subscribed SQS queue, so each consumer gets its own independent stream.\n* **Buffering per consumer:** Each SQS queue retains messages (up to 14 days) while its consumer is slow or offline for maintenance.\n* **Filtering without code:** Subscription filter policies on message attributes or the message body let SNS deliver only matching events to the reorder and pricing queues, with no filtering logic in the consumers.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option A is incorrect:** With a single queue, consumers compete for messages; once one consumer deletes a message, the others never receive it. This does not give each service a copy.\n* **Option B is incorrect:** Scanning a table on a schedule is inefficient, adds latency, and requires custom tracking of which items each consumer has already processed.\n* **Option C is incorrect:** Point-to-point synchronous calls tightly couple the producer to every consumer, and events are lost if a consumer is offline longer than the retry window.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **\"Multiple consumers each need a copy\":** SNS topic fan-out to multiple SQS queues.\n2. **\"Consumers only need a subset\":** Use SNS subscription filter policies instead of filtering in application code."
    },
    {
      "domain": 2,
      "task": "2.1",
      "topics": [
        "AWS Step Functions",
        "AWS Lambda"
      ],
      "prompt": "A financial services company is building a loan-approval process. The workflow runs several AWS Lambda functions in sequence, branches based on a credit score, retries transient failures automatically, and must pause until a human underwriter approves or rejects the application through an internal web portal. Underwriter approval can take up to 5 days. The company wants a fully managed solution with built-in visual tracking of each application's progress. Which solution meets these requirements?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Use an AWS Step Functions Standard workflow with Choice and Retry states and a .waitForTaskToken task whose token the portal returns when the underwriter decides.",
        "B": "Use an AWS Step Functions Express workflow with Choice and Retry states, and add a Wait state loop that polls an Amazon DynamoDB table every hour for the underwriter's decision.",
        "C": "Chain the Lambda functions together by having each function invoke the next one asynchronously, and store the application state in an Amazon S3 object between steps.",
        "D": "Use a single Lambda function that orchestrates all steps and sleeps until the underwriter's decision is written to an Amazon DynamoDB table."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "A"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option A is Correct\n\n* **Long-running orchestration:** Standard workflows can run for up to one year, so a 5-day human approval step is supported.\n* **Callback pattern:** The .waitForTaskToken integration pauses the execution until the portal calls SendTaskSuccess or SendTaskFailure with the task token, with no polling required.\n* **Built-in resilience and visibility:** Retry/Catch and Choice states are declarative, and the console shows a visual execution history for every application.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option B is incorrect:** Express workflows have a maximum duration of 5 minutes and do not support the .waitForTaskToken callback pattern, so they cannot wait days for a human decision.\n* **Option C is incorrect:** Chaining functions manually tightly couples them, requires custom retry and state handling, and provides no centralized visual tracking.\n* **Option D is incorrect:** Lambda functions have a maximum timeout of 15 minutes, so a single function cannot wait days for approval.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **Human approval / external callback:** Step Functions Standard workflow + .waitForTaskToken.\n2. **Standard vs Express:** Standard = long-running (up to 1 year), exactly-once, auditable. Express = high-volume, short (up to 5 minutes) event processing."
    },
    {
      "domain": 2,
      "task": "2.1",
      "topics": [
        "Amazon RDS",
        "Read replicas"
      ],
      "prompt": "A company runs an Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL DB instance that supports an online order system. The business intelligence team runs long, read-only reporting queries against the same database during business hours, which increases latency for customer transactions. The reports can tolerate data that is a few seconds old. The company wants to resolve the performance impact with minimal changes to the order system. Which solution meets these requirements?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Convert the DB instance to a Multi-AZ deployment and point the reporting tools at the standby instance.",
        "B": "Increase the allocated storage and switch the DB instance to Provisioned IOPS so that both workloads have more I/O capacity.",
        "C": "Create an RDS read replica of the DB instance and configure the reporting tools to use the read replica's endpoint.",
        "D": "Take a manual snapshot each morning, restore it to a new DB instance, and have the reporting tools query the restored instance."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "C"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option C is Correct\n\n* **Offload read traffic:** A read replica receives asynchronous updates from the primary and serves read-only queries, removing the reporting load from the primary.\n* **Near-real-time data:** Replication lag is typically seconds, which meets the stated tolerance.\n* **Minimal change:** Only the reporting tools' connection string changes; the order system continues to use the primary endpoint.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option A is incorrect:** In a Multi-AZ DB instance deployment, the standby cannot serve read traffic. Multi-AZ provides high availability, not read scaling.\n* **Option B is incorrect:** More I/O capacity does not isolate the workloads; long reporting queries still compete for CPU, memory, and locks on the primary.\n* **Option D is incorrect:** A daily restore produces data that is up to a day old, which does not meet the requirement for data that is only seconds old, and adds operational work.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **\"Read-heavy / reporting queries slowing the primary\":** Use read replicas (RDS or Aurora).\n2. **Multi-AZ vs read replica:** Multi-AZ DB instance = synchronous standby for failover (not readable). Read replica = asynchronous copy for read scaling."
    },
    {
      "domain": 2,
      "task": "2.1",
      "topics": [
        "Amazon ECS",
        "AWS Fargate",
        "Elastic Load Balancing",
        "Amazon ECR"
      ],
      "prompt": "A company wants to migrate a monolithic Java web application from on-premises virtual machines to containers on AWS. The operations team is small and does not want to patch, scale, or manage servers or a container control plane. The application must scale horizontally based on load and must be reachable over HTTPS. Which solution meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Build a container image, store it in Amazon ECR, and run it as an Amazon ECS service on AWS Fargate behind an Application Load Balancer with ECS Service Auto Scaling.",
        "B": "Build a container image, store it in Amazon ECR, and deploy it to a self-managed Kubernetes cluster on Amazon EC2 instances behind an Application Load Balancer.",
        "C": "Run the container image with Docker on Amazon EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling group behind an Application Load Balancer, and use user data scripts to pull and start the container.",
        "D": "Rewrite the application as AWS Lambda functions and expose them through Amazon API Gateway."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "A"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option A is Correct\n\n* **Serverless containers:** With Fargate, AWS provisions and patches the underlying compute; the team manages only task definitions.\n* **Managed orchestration:** Amazon ECS has no control plane to operate, and ECS Service Auto Scaling adds or removes tasks based on metrics such as CPU or ALB request count.\n* **HTTPS access:** An Application Load Balancer with an ACM certificate terminates HTTPS and distributes traffic across tasks in multiple Availability Zones.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option B is incorrect:** A self-managed Kubernetes cluster requires the team to operate the control plane and worker nodes, which is the opposite of the requirement.\n* **Option C is incorrect:** The team must still patch and manage EC2 instances and build its own container lifecycle management.\n* **Option D is incorrect:** Rewriting a monolith into Lambda functions is a re-architecture, not a container migration, and requires significant development effort.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **\"Containers without managing servers\":** Amazon ECS (or EKS) on AWS Fargate.\n2. **Least overhead for containers:** ECS on Fargate is typically the lowest-overhead choice unless the scenario explicitly requires Kubernetes, in which case use Amazon EKS."
    },
    {
      "domain": 2,
      "task": "2.1",
      "topics": [
        "Amazon CloudFront",
        "Amazon S3",
        "Elastic Load Balancing"
      ],
      "prompt": "A media company serves a global audience from a single AWS Region. Static assets (images, JavaScript, CSS) are stored in an Amazon S3 bucket, and dynamic API requests are served by Amazon EC2 instances behind an Application Load Balancer. Users far from the Region report slow page loads, and the company wants to reduce load on the origin. The S3 bucket must not be publicly accessible. Which solution meets these requirements?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration on the bucket and configure AWS Global Accelerator in front of the Application Load Balancer.",
        "B": "Enable static website hosting on the S3 bucket with a public bucket policy, and create a CloudFront distribution with the ALB as its only origin.",
        "C": "Create a CloudFront distribution with the ALB as the default origin and a /static/* behavior that uses the S3 bucket as an origin with origin access control (OAC).",
        "D": "Configure S3 Cross-Region Replication to buckets in three other Regions, make those buckets publicly readable, and use Amazon Route 53 latency-based routing to send users to the closest bucket."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "C"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option C is Correct\n\n* **Edge caching:** CloudFront caches static content at edge locations close to users, reducing latency and origin load, and accelerates dynamic requests over the AWS backbone.\n* **Multiple origins:** Cache behaviors route path patterns to different origins, so one distribution serves both the S3 assets and the ALB API.\n* **Private bucket:** Origin access control (OAC) signs requests to S3 so the bucket can stay private with Block Public Access enabled; OAC is the recommended replacement for the legacy origin access identity (OAI).\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option A is incorrect:** Transfer Acceleration speeds up uploads and downloads over long distances but does not cache content, and Global Accelerator does not cache either, so origin load is not reduced.\n* **Option B is incorrect:** Making the bucket public violates the requirement, and the static assets would not be served through CloudFront.\n* **Option D is incorrect:** Replicating to multiple Regions adds cost and complexity, still requires public or separately secured buckets, and does not cache content or accelerate the dynamic API.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **\"Global users + static content + reduce origin load\":** Amazon CloudFront.\n2. **Private S3 origin for CloudFront:** Use origin access control (OAC), not public buckets and not the legacy OAI."
    },
    {
      "domain": 2,
      "task": "2.1",
      "topics": [
        "Amazon API Gateway"
      ],
      "prompt": "A company exposes a public REST API through Amazon API Gateway for third-party partners. Each partner has a contract that defines a maximum request rate and a monthly request quota. The company must enforce these limits per partner and identify which partner sent each request, without writing custom throttling code. Which solution meets these requirements?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Create an API Gateway HTTP API and configure route-level throttling for each route.",
        "B": "Create an API Gateway REST API. Issue an API key to each partner and associate the keys with usage plans that define the throttling limits and quotas for each contract tier.",
        "C": "Attach an AWS WAF web ACL with a rate-based rule to the API stage for each partner.",
        "D": "Configure a Lambda authorizer that records each partner's request count in an Amazon DynamoDB table and denies requests from partners that exceed their contracted rate or monthly quota."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "B"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option B is Correct\n\n* **Usage plans:** API Gateway REST API usage plans define a throttle (rate and burst) and a quota (requests per day, week, or month).\n* **Per-client identification:** API keys associate each request with a specific partner and the usage plan that applies to that partner.\n* **No custom code:** Throttling and quota enforcement are handled by API Gateway, which returns HTTP 429 when limits are exceeded.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option A is incorrect:** HTTP APIs do not support API keys or usage plans. Route-level throttling applies to all callers, not to each partner.\n* **Option C is incorrect:** WAF rate-based rules limit requests per aggregation key over a short window; they do not provide monthly quotas or per-partner contract tiers.\n* **Option D is incorrect:** This requires custom code and adds latency and cost, which the requirement explicitly wants to avoid.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **\"Per-customer throttling and quotas\":** API Gateway REST API + API keys + usage plans.\n2. **REST API vs HTTP API:** HTTP APIs are cheaper and simpler, but features such as usage plans, API keys, and request validation require REST APIs."
    },
    {
      "domain": 2,
      "task": "2.1",
      "topics": [
        "AWS Transfer Family",
        "Amazon S3"
      ],
      "prompt": "A logistics company receives shipment files from dozens of external partners who upload them with SFTP to a server in the company's data center. The company is moving to AWS and wants the files to land directly in Amazon S3 for downstream processing. Partners must continue to use their existing SFTP clients and hostnames with no changes on their side, other than an updated DNS record managed by the company. The company wants to avoid managing servers. Which solution meets these requirements?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Deploy an SFTP server on Amazon EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling group behind a Network Load Balancer, and use a cron job on each instance to copy uploaded files to Amazon S3.",
        "B": "Deploy AWS Storage Gateway S3 File Gateway on premises and have partners upload to the gateway through SMB.",
        "C": "Keep the on-premises SFTP server and use AWS DataSync to copy its upload directory, shared over NFS, to Amazon S3 on a schedule.",
        "D": "Create an AWS Transfer Family SFTP server with Amazon S3 as the storage backend, configure partner users, and point the company's custom hostname to the server endpoint."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "D"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option D is Correct\n\n* **Fully managed SFTP:** AWS Transfer Family provides a managed, highly available SFTP endpoint, so there are no servers to patch or scale.\n* **Direct to S3:** Uploaded files are written directly to the S3 bucket, where they can trigger downstream processing through S3 Event Notifications or EventBridge.\n* **No partner changes:** Partners keep using SFTP; the company points its custom DNS name at the Transfer Family endpoint.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option A is incorrect:** Self-managed EC2 SFTP servers require patching, scaling, and custom copy scripts.\n* **Option B is incorrect:** Partners would have to switch from SFTP to SMB and reach an on-premises appliance, which violates the requirement.\n* **Option C is incorrect:** This keeps the on-premises SFTP server in place, so the company does not leave its data center, and files arrive in S3 only on a schedule.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **\"SFTP / FTPS / FTP / AS2 into S3 or EFS\":** AWS Transfer Family.\n2. **Managed services over self-managed:** When a scenario says to avoid managing servers, prefer the purpose-built managed service."
    },
    {
      "domain": 2,
      "task": "2.1",
      "topics": [
        "Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling",
        "Amazon ElastiCache",
        "Amazon S3",
        "Elastic Load Balancing"
      ],
      "prompt": "A company runs a web application on a fixed group of four Amazon EC2 instances behind an Application Load Balancer with sticky sessions enabled. User session data is stored in each instance's memory, and user-uploaded profile images are written to each instance's local disk. When instances are replaced, users are logged out and images disappear. The company wants to make the web tier stateless so that it can scale horizontally in an Auto Scaling group. Which combination of steps should a solutions architect take? (Select TWO.)",
      "options": {
        "A": "Increase the sticky session duration on the target group to 7 days.",
        "B": "Store session data in an Amazon ElastiCache cluster that all instances share.",
        "C": "Store user-uploaded images on an Amazon EBS volume attached to the first instance and share it with the other instances through NFS.",
        "D": "Move the instances to a cluster placement group so they can share memory more efficiently.",
        "E": "Store user-uploaded images in Amazon S3 and save the object key in the application database."
      },
      "isMulti": true,
      "requiredCount": 2,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "B",
        "E"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Options B & E are Correct\n\n* **Externalize session state (B):** A shared in-memory store such as ElastiCache (Valkey, Redis OSS, or Memcached) lets any instance serve any request, so instances can be added or terminated without logging users out.\n* **Externalize files (E):** Amazon S3 provides durable, shared object storage that is independent of any instance's lifecycle.\n* **Stateless design:** With no state on the instances, sticky sessions are no longer needed and the Auto Scaling group can scale in and out freely.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option A is incorrect:** Longer stickiness keeps state on individual instances; sessions are still lost when an instance is terminated.\n* **Option C is incorrect:** This creates a single point of failure on one instance in one Availability Zone and does not scale.\n* **Option D is incorrect:** Placement groups affect network latency between instances; they do not share memory or externalize state.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **Stateless web tier:** Move sessions to ElastiCache or DynamoDB and files to S3 or EFS.\n2. **Sticky sessions:** Sticky sessions are a workaround for stateful apps; for horizontal scaling and resilience, externalize state instead."
    },
    {
      "domain": 2,
      "task": "2.2",
      "topics": [
        "Amazon Aurora",
        "Aurora Global Database"
      ],
      "prompt": "A gaming company runs its player-profile database on Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL in us-east-1. The company's DR policy requires that, if the entire Region becomes unavailable, the database must be available in us-west-2 with an RPO of about 1 second and an RTO of less than 1 minute. Players in us-west-2 should also be able to read profile data with low latency during normal operation. Which solution meets these requirements?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Convert the cluster to an Aurora Global Database with a secondary cluster in us-west-2, and use a managed failover to promote the secondary cluster during a Regional outage.",
        "B": "Enable automated backups and copy snapshots to us-west-2 every hour with AWS Backup. Restore the latest snapshot during an outage.",
        "C": "Add two Aurora Replicas in different Availability Zones in us-east-1 and enable Multi-AZ failover.",
        "D": "Use AWS DMS with ongoing replication to an Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL DB instance in us-west-2, and update the application connection string during an outage."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "A"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option A is Correct\n\n* **Storage-level replication:** Aurora Global Database replicates using dedicated infrastructure with typical lag under 1 second, meeting an RPO of about 1 second.\n* **Fast cross-Region recovery:** A secondary cluster can be promoted in under a minute, and managed failover handles the promotion and topology changes.\n* **Local reads:** The secondary cluster in us-west-2 serves low-latency reads during normal operation.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option B is incorrect:** Hourly snapshot copies give an RPO of up to an hour, and restoring a snapshot takes far longer than 1 minute.\n* **Option C is incorrect:** Aurora Replicas in one Region protect against instance or AZ failure, not against the loss of an entire Region.\n* **Option D is incorrect:** DMS replication lag is typically higher, and promoting a different engine target plus manual connection changes will not reliably meet a sub-minute RTO.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **\"Cross-Region, RPO ~1 s, RTO < 1 min, relational\":** Aurora Global Database.\n2. **Scope of protection:** Aurora Replicas / Multi-AZ = AZ failure. Global Database = Regional failure."
    },
    {
      "domain": 2,
      "task": "2.2",
      "topics": [
        "Disaster recovery",
        "Amazon RDS",
        "AWS CloudFormation"
      ],
      "prompt": "A company runs a three-tier application in a single AWS Region with an Amazon RDS for MySQL database. The company needs a disaster recovery plan for a Regional outage with an RPO of 1 minute and an RTO of 4 hours. To minimize cost, no application or web servers may run in the recovery Region until a disaster is declared. Which DR strategy meets these requirements MOST cost-effectively?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Backup and restore: take daily automated snapshots, copy them to the recovery Region, and rebuild the environment from the snapshots during a disaster.",
        "B": "Warm standby: run a scaled-down but fully functional copy of the web and application tiers, plus a replicated copy of the database, in the recovery Region, and scale the tiers up when a disaster is declared.",
        "C": "Pilot light: keep a cross-Region read replica of the database plus AMIs and AWS CloudFormation templates in the recovery Region; launch the application tiers and promote the replica in a disaster.",
        "D": "Multi-site active-active: run the full application in both Regions behind Amazon Route 53 latency-based routing with bidirectional database replication."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "C"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option C is Correct\n\n* **Core data always on:** In a pilot light strategy, only the data tier is continuously replicated, so the RPO is limited to replication lag (well under 1 minute).\n* **Compute off until needed:** Application servers are not running; they are provisioned from AMIs and infrastructure as code when a disaster is declared, which fits within a 4-hour RTO.\n* **Lowest cost meeting the RPO:** Paying only for the replica and stored artifacts is cheaper than warm standby or active-active.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option A is incorrect:** Daily snapshots give an RPO of up to 24 hours, which does not meet the 1-minute RPO.\n* **Option B is incorrect:** Warm standby runs application servers in the recovery Region at all times, which violates the requirement and costs more.\n* **Option D is incorrect:** Active-active is the most expensive strategy and runs full capacity in both Regions, far exceeding the stated RTO and cost requirements.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **DR strategies by cost and RTO/RPO:** Backup & restore (hours) < Pilot light (core data live, compute off) < Warm standby (scaled-down copy running) < Multi-site active-active (near zero).\n2. **Pilot light keyword:** \"Data replicated, servers not running until failover\" indicates pilot light."
    },
    {
      "domain": 2,
      "task": "2.2",
      "topics": [
        "Amazon RDS Proxy",
        "AWS Lambda",
        "Amazon Aurora"
      ],
      "prompt": "A company has a serverless API built with AWS Lambda functions that query an Amazon Aurora MySQL DB cluster. During traffic bursts, thousands of concurrent Lambda invocations open new database connections, causing \"too many connections\" errors. During a recent Aurora failover, the application also experienced several minutes of errors because clients cached the old writer's DNS address. Which solution addresses both problems with the LEAST application change?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Increase the max_connections parameter in the DB cluster parameter group and reduce the DNS TTL in the Lambda function code.",
        "B": "Create an Amazon RDS Proxy for the Aurora cluster and configure the Lambda functions to connect to the proxy endpoint.",
        "C": "Set reserved concurrency on the Lambda functions to 10 and add Aurora Replicas to absorb the load.",
        "D": "Place an Amazon SQS queue between Amazon API Gateway and the Lambda functions so that requests are processed one at a time."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "B"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option B is Correct\n\n* **Connection pooling:** RDS Proxy maintains a pool of database connections and multiplexes many Lambda client connections over them, preventing connection exhaustion.\n* **Faster failover:** The proxy detects failover and routes to the new writer without relying on client DNS caching, reducing failover time for Aurora and RDS (by up to 66% according to AWS).\n* **Minimal change:** The only application change is the connection endpoint; the proxy can also use Secrets Manager or IAM authentication.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option A is incorrect:** Raising max_connections consumes more memory and still has a ceiling, and changing DNS behavior in code does not reliably fix failover handling.\n* **Option C is incorrect:** Heavily limiting concurrency throttles the API and causes errors for users; Aurora Replicas serve reads but do not reduce writer connections.\n* **Option D is incorrect:** Making a synchronous API asynchronous changes the API's behavior for clients and does not address failover.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **\"Lambda + relational DB + too many connections\":** Amazon RDS Proxy.\n2. **Proxy concepts:** RDS Proxy also improves availability by reducing failover time and preserving application connections."
    },
    {
      "domain": 2,
      "task": "2.2",
      "topics": [
        "Amazon Route 53",
        "Amazon S3",
        "Health checks"
      ],
      "prompt": "A company hosts its public website on Amazon EC2 instances behind an Application Load Balancer in one AWS Region. The company wants visitors to see a static \"maintenance\" page hosted in Amazon S3 automatically whenever the primary site is unhealthy, and to return to the primary site automatically when it recovers. Which solution meets these requirements?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Create Route 53 weighted records that send 90% of traffic to the ALB and 10% of traffic to the S3 website endpoint.",
        "B": "Create a Route 53 latency-based routing policy with one record for the ALB and one record for the S3 website endpoint.",
        "C": "Configure the ALB listener with a fixed-response action that returns the maintenance page whenever all registered targets are unhealthy, and store the maintenance page in Amazon S3.",
        "D": "Create Route 53 failover routing records: a primary alias record for the ALB with Evaluate Target Health enabled, and a secondary alias record for the S3 static website endpoint."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "D"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option D is Correct\n\n* **Active-passive failover:** Failover routing returns the primary record while it is healthy and the secondary record only when the primary is unhealthy.\n* **Automatic failback:** When health checks pass again, Route 53 automatically resumes answering with the primary record.\n* **Health evaluation:** With an alias record to the ALB, Evaluate Target Health makes the record healthy only when the ALB has healthy targets.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option A is incorrect:** Weighted routing would send 10% of users to the maintenance page even when the primary site is healthy.\n* **Option B is incorrect:** Latency-based routing selects the lowest-latency record, not the healthy one, so users could reach the maintenance page while the primary is healthy.\n* **Option C is incorrect:** ALB fixed-response actions return a static string defined in the listener rule; they cannot fetch content from S3, and this does not help if the ALB itself is unavailable.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **\"Active-passive\" / \"failover to static site\":** Route 53 failover routing policy with health checks.\n2. **Routing policies:** Weighted = split traffic; latency = performance; geolocation = compliance/localization; failover = DR."
    },
    {
      "domain": 2,
      "task": "2.2",
      "topics": [
        "Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling",
        "Availability Zones",
        "Legacy applications"
      ],
      "prompt": "A company runs a legacy, stateless licensing service on a single Amazon EC2 instance. The vendor license allows only one running instance at a time, and the application code cannot be modified. The service must recover automatically if the instance fails or if its Availability Zone becomes unavailable. Which solution meets these requirements?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Create a launch template from the instance's AMI and an Auto Scaling group with minimum, maximum, and desired capacity of 1 that spans multiple Availability Zones.",
        "B": "Create an Amazon CloudWatch alarm on the StatusCheckFailed_System metric with an EC2 recover action.",
        "C": "Launch a second instance from the same AMI in another Availability Zone and keep it running as a hot standby behind a Network Load Balancer with cross-zone load balancing.",
        "D": "Enable termination protection and take hourly EBS snapshots with Amazon Data Lifecycle Manager."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "A"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option A is Correct\n\n* **Self-healing:** The Auto Scaling group replaces the instance automatically when it fails health checks, keeping exactly one instance running.\n* **AZ resilience:** Because the group spans multiple Availability Zones, a replacement instance is launched in a healthy AZ if the original AZ is impaired.\n* **No code change:** The same AMI is used, so the legacy application runs unchanged.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option B is incorrect:** EC2 automatic recovery keeps the instance in the same Availability Zone, so it does not protect against an AZ failure.\n* **Option C is incorrect:** Running a second instance violates the single-instance license.\n* **Option D is incorrect:** Snapshots protect data but do not recover the service automatically; termination protection does not prevent failures.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **\"Single instance must self-heal across AZs\":** Auto Scaling group with min = max = desired = 1 across multiple AZs.\n2. **Recover action scope:** CloudWatch/EC2 automatic recovery handles underlying host failures in the same AZ only."
    },
    {
      "domain": 2,
      "task": "2.2",
      "topics": [
        "Service Quotas",
        "Disaster recovery",
        "AWS Regions"
      ],
      "prompt": "A company uses a warm standby DR strategy. The production Region runs 400 vCPUs of On-Demand EC2 instances, while the standby Region runs only 32 vCPUs. During a recent DR test, the Auto Scaling group in the standby Region could not scale out because launches failed with a vCPU limit error. The company must ensure that the standby Region can scale to full production capacity during a failover. What should a solutions architect do?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Create an Auto Scaling group in the production Region that launches instances into the standby Region's subnets during failover.",
        "B": "Purchase Compute Savings Plans for 400 vCPUs so that capacity is reserved in the standby Region.",
        "C": "Use Service Quotas to request an increase of the relevant Running On-Demand instances vCPU quota in the standby Region to at least production levels, and verify it during DR tests.",
        "D": "Enable AWS Organizations and apply a service control policy that removes the EC2 vCPU limit for the account."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "C"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option C is Correct\n\n* **Quotas are Regional:** EC2 On-Demand vCPU quotas apply per Region per account, so a quota sufficient in production does not carry over to the standby Region.\n* **Plan ahead:** Requesting increases through Service Quotas before a disaster ensures the standby environment can scale to full capacity when needed.\n* **Validate:** Regular DR tests that scale to full capacity confirm the quota and the scaling configuration actually work.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option A is incorrect:** Auto Scaling groups are Regional and cannot launch instances into subnets in another Region.\n* **Option B is incorrect:** Savings Plans are a pricing commitment; they do not reserve capacity or raise service quotas.\n* **Option D is incorrect:** SCPs can only restrict permissions; they cannot raise service quotas.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **Standby environments and quotas:** Raise service quotas in the DR Region in advance; quotas are per account per Region.\n2. **Capacity vs pricing:** On-Demand Capacity Reservations reserve capacity; Savings Plans and Reserved Instances (Regional) only provide discounts."
    },
    {
      "domain": 2,
      "task": "2.2",
      "topics": [
        "Amazon VPC",
        "NAT gateway",
        "Amazon RDS Multi-AZ"
      ],
      "prompt": "A company runs an application on EC2 instances in private subnets in two Availability Zones behind an Application Load Balancer. All instances reach the internet for software updates through a single NAT gateway in the first AZ's public subnet, using a shared private route table. The application uses a Single-AZ Amazon RDS for MySQL DB instance in the first AZ. The company wants to remove single points of failure so that the application keeps working if either AZ fails. Which combination of steps should a solutions architect take? (Select TWO.)",
      "options": {
        "A": "Create a NAT gateway in a public subnet in each AZ, and give each AZ's private subnets their own route table that points to the NAT gateway in the same AZ.",
        "B": "Replace the NAT gateway with a NAT instance on a larger EC2 instance type.",
        "C": "Create an RDS read replica in the same Availability Zone as the primary DB instance.",
        "D": "Convert the RDS DB instance to a Multi-AZ deployment.",
        "E": "Place the EC2 instances in a spread placement group within the first Availability Zone."
      },
      "isMulti": true,
      "requiredCount": 2,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "A",
        "D"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Options A & D are Correct\n\n* **NAT gateways are zonal (A):** A NAT gateway is redundant only within its own AZ. One NAT gateway per AZ, with AZ-specific route tables, means an AZ outage affects only that AZ's traffic.\n* **Database failover (D):** A Multi-AZ deployment keeps a synchronous standby in another AZ and fails over automatically, typically within one to two minutes for a Multi-AZ DB instance.\n* **Remove SPOFs:** Together these changes ensure that both the egress path and the database survive the loss of either AZ.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option B is incorrect:** A NAT instance is a single EC2 instance in one AZ, so it is still a single point of failure and adds management overhead.\n* **Option C is incorrect:** A read replica in the same AZ is lost along with the primary if that AZ fails, and it is not an automatic failover target.\n* **Option E is incorrect:** Keeping all instances in one AZ increases exposure to an AZ failure; spread placement groups only separate instances across racks.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **NAT gateway HA:** Deploy one NAT gateway per AZ and route each AZ's private subnets to its local NAT gateway.\n2. **RDS HA vs scaling:** Multi-AZ for availability, read replicas for read scaling (a cross-AZ or cross-Region replica can be promoted manually for DR)."
    },
    {
      "domain": 2,
      "task": "2.2",
      "topics": [
        "Amazon S3",
        "S3 Object Lock",
        "S3 Cross-Region Replication",
        "S3 Versioning"
      ],
      "prompt": "A healthcare company stores medical imaging files in an Amazon S3 bucket in eu-west-1. Compliance rules require that: previous versions of files can be recovered if they are overwritten; a copy of every file exists in a second AWS Region; and no user, including the root user, can delete or overwrite any file version for 7 years after it is written. Which combination of actions meets these requirements? (Select THREE.)",
      "options": {
        "A": "Enable S3 Versioning on the source bucket and on a destination bucket in another Region.",
        "B": "Configure S3 Lifecycle rules to transition objects to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after 30 days.",
        "C": "Configure S3 Cross-Region Replication from the source bucket to the destination bucket in the other Region.",
        "D": "Enable S3 Object Lock in governance mode with a 7-year default retention period.",
        "E": "Configure S3 Same-Region Replication to a second bucket in eu-west-1.",
        "F": "Enable S3 Object Lock in compliance mode with a 7-year default retention period on both buckets."
      },
      "isMulti": true,
      "requiredCount": 3,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "A",
        "C",
        "F"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Options A, C & F are Correct\n\n* **Versioning (A):** Versioning preserves every version of an object so overwritten data can be recovered, and it is a prerequisite for both replication and Object Lock.\n* **Cross-Region Replication (C):** CRR asynchronously copies new objects to a bucket in another Region, providing a geographically separate copy.\n* **Compliance mode (F):** In compliance mode, no user, including the root user, can overwrite or delete a protected object version or shorten its retention period until the retention period expires.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option B is incorrect:** Storage class transitions reduce cost but do not provide versioning, Regional copies, or immutability.\n* **Option D is incorrect:** Governance mode can be bypassed by users with the s3:BypassGovernanceRetention permission, so it does not prevent all users from deleting objects.\n* **Option E is incorrect:** Same-Region Replication keeps the copy in the same Region, which does not satisfy the requirement for a copy in a second Region.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **WORM / immutable storage on S3:** Object Lock compliance mode = nobody can delete, including root; governance mode = privileged users can bypass.\n2. **Replication prerequisites:** CRR and SRR require versioning on both source and destination buckets."
    }
