    {
      "domain": 2,
      "task": "2.1",
      "topics": [
        "Amazon EventBridge",
        "AWS Lambda",
        "Amazon SQS"
      ],
      "prompt": "A company uses a SaaS customer-support platform that is an Amazon EventBridge partner. The company wants to react to ticket events in near real time: high-priority tickets must invoke an AWS Lambda function that pages the on-call engineer, and all ticket events must be sent to an Amazon SQS queue for analytics. The company wants to avoid writing and hosting integration code to receive events from the SaaS platform. Which solution meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Create an Amazon API Gateway endpoint that receives webhooks from the SaaS platform and invokes a Lambda function that forwards events to the target Lambda function and the SQS queue.",
        "B": "Write a scheduled Lambda function that polls the SaaS platform's API every minute and publishes new tickets to an Amazon SNS topic.",
        "C": "Associate the SaaS partner event source with an EventBridge partner event bus, and create rules that match high-priority ticket events to the Lambda function and all ticket events to the SQS queue.",
        "D": "Configure the SaaS platform to write events into an Amazon Kinesis Data Streams stream, and use Lambda consumers to filter and route the records."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "C"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option C is Correct\n\n* **Native SaaS integration:** EventBridge partner event sources deliver events from supported SaaS providers directly to a partner event bus in the customer's account, with no integration code to host.\n* **Content-based routing:** Rules use event patterns to match fields such as priority and route matching events to targets such as Lambda functions and SQS queues.\n* **Loose coupling:** New consumers can be added later as new rules or targets without changing the producer.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option A is incorrect:** This requires building, securing, and maintaining a webhook endpoint and forwarding code.\n* **Option B is incorrect:** Polling adds latency, consumes API quotas, and requires custom code to track which tickets have already been processed.\n* **Option D is incorrect:** The SaaS platform would need custom code to write to Kinesis, and the Lambda consumers must implement routing logic.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **\"SaaS events into AWS\":** Amazon EventBridge partner event sources (or Amazon AppFlow for bulk data transfer).\n2. **Event-driven routing:** EventBridge rules with event patterns route events by content to many target types."
    },
    {
      "domain": 2,
      "task": "2.1",
      "topics": [
        "Amazon SQS FIFO"
      ],
      "prompt": "A bank processes account transactions through a queue. Transactions for the same account must be processed strictly in the order they were sent, and a transaction must never be processed twice even if the producer retries a send after a network timeout. Transactions for different accounts should be processed in parallel by multiple consumers to maximize throughput. Which solution meets these requirements?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Use an Amazon SQS FIFO queue, set the message group ID to the account ID, and supply a message deduplication ID (or enable content-based deduplication).",
        "B": "Use an Amazon SQS standard queue and include a sequence number and a unique transaction ID in each message so that consumers can reorder and deduplicate messages.",
        "C": "Use an Amazon SQS FIFO queue with a single message group ID for all messages so that ordering is preserved globally.",
        "D": "Use an Amazon SNS standard topic with an SQS standard queue subscription and enable raw message delivery."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "A"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option A is Correct\n\n* **Per-group ordering:** SQS FIFO guarantees strict ordering within a message group; using the account ID as the group ID orders each account's transactions.\n* **Parallelism across groups:** Different message groups can be consumed concurrently by multiple consumers, so throughput scales with the number of accounts.\n* **Deduplication:** A deduplication ID (or content-based deduplication) causes duplicate sends within the 5-minute deduplication interval to be accepted but not delivered again.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option B is incorrect:** Standard queues provide at-least-once delivery and best-effort ordering, so duplicates can occur and consumers would need complex reordering logic.\n* **Option C is incorrect:** A single message group forces all messages to be processed one at a time, preventing parallel processing across accounts.\n* **Option D is incorrect:** Standard SNS topics and standard SQS queues do not guarantee ordering or exactly-once processing.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **\"Order matters + no duplicates\":** SQS FIFO (or SNS FIFO for fan-out).\n2. **Message group ID:** Choose a group ID that matches the ordering scope (for example, per customer or per account) to keep parallelism."
    },
    {
      "domain": 2,
      "task": "2.1",
      "topics": [
        "AWS Fargate",
        "Amazon ECS",
        "Amazon EventBridge Scheduler"
      ],
      "prompt": "A company has a containerized nightly batch job that reconciles financial records. The job runs for about 2 hours, needs 8 vCPUs and 32 GB of memory, and should run every night at 01:00 UTC. The company does not want to manage any servers or keep compute running between runs. Which solution meets these requirements with the LEAST operational overhead?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Package the job as an AWS Lambda function configured with the maximum 10,240 MB of memory and the maximum timeout, and invoke it nightly with an Amazon EventBridge Scheduler schedule.",
        "B": "Run the job on an Amazon EC2 instance that is always on and use a cron entry to start the container each night.",
        "C": "Package the job as an AWS Lambda container image with Lambda SnapStart enabled and invoke it from a Step Functions Express workflow.",
        "D": "Create an Amazon ECS task definition for AWS Fargate with 8 vCPUs and 32 GB of memory, and use Amazon EventBridge Scheduler to run the task every night at 01:00 UTC."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "D"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option D is Correct\n\n* **No time limit:** Fargate tasks can run for as long as needed, unlike Lambda's 15-minute maximum.\n* **Right-sized serverless compute:** Fargate supports task sizes up to 16 vCPUs and 120 GB of memory, and an 8 vCPU task can use 16-60 GB of memory in 4 GB increments, so 8 vCPUs and 32 GB is supported, and you pay only while the task runs.\n* **Managed scheduling:** EventBridge Scheduler can invoke the ECS RunTask API directly on a cron expression with built-in retries.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option A is incorrect:** Lambda functions have a maximum timeout of 15 minutes, so a 2-hour job cannot run in Lambda.\n* **Option B is incorrect:** An always-on instance must be patched and managed and incurs cost between runs.\n* **Option C is incorrect:** SnapStart reduces cold-start latency but does not extend Lambda's 15-minute limit, and Express workflows have a 5-minute maximum duration.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **Lambda vs Fargate:** Use Lambda for short (≤ 15 min) event-driven code; use Fargate for longer-running or larger containerized tasks without servers.\n2. **Scheduled jobs:** EventBridge Scheduler supports cron and rate expressions and many target types, including ECS tasks."
    },
    {
      "domain": 2,
      "task": "2.1",
      "topics": [
        "Amazon Kinesis Data Streams"
      ],
      "prompt": "An e-commerce company captures clickstream events from its website. Three independent applications (fraud detection, personalization, and a data lake loader) must each read every event with sub-second latency. Events for the same user session must be read in order, and any application must be able to reprocess the last 7 days of events after a bug fix. Which service should a solutions architect use to ingest the events?",
      "options": {
        "A": "An Amazon SQS standard queue that all three applications poll.",
        "B": "Amazon Kinesis Data Streams with the session ID as the partition key and a data retention period of 7 days.",
        "C": "Amazon Data Firehose delivering the events to an Amazon S3 bucket that all three applications read.",
        "D": "An Amazon SNS standard topic with a separate Amazon SQS standard queue subscribed for each of the three applications."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "B"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option B is Correct\n\n* **Multiple independent consumers:** Each application reads the stream independently with its own position (and can use enhanced fan-out for dedicated throughput).\n* **Ordering:** Records with the same partition key go to the same shard and are read in order.\n* **Replay:** Retention can be extended up to 365 days; consumers can re-read from any point in the retention window.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option A is incorrect:** SQS consumers compete for messages, so each event is processed by only one application, and processed messages are deleted and cannot be replayed.\n* **Option C is incorrect:** Firehose buffers data before delivery (seconds to minutes), so it does not meet sub-second latency, and it does not provide per-session ordering for consumers.\n* **Option D is incorrect:** SNS with SQS gives each application a copy, but standard queues do not guarantee ordering and messages cannot be replayed after they are deleted.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **\"Multiple consumers + ordering + replay\":** Amazon Kinesis Data Streams (or Amazon MSK).\n2. **SQS vs Kinesis:** SQS = per-message work queue, deleted after processing. Kinesis = ordered, replayable stream read by many consumers."
    },
    {
      "domain": 2,
      "task": "2.1",
      "topics": [
        "Amazon FSx for Windows File Server",
        "Storage types"
      ],
      "prompt": "A company is migrating a .NET application that runs on Windows Server to Amazon EC2 instances in two Availability Zones. The application instances must share files through SMB, and file access must be controlled with the company's existing Microsoft Active Directory users and groups. The shared storage must remain available if one AZ fails. Which storage solution meets these requirements?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Amazon EFS with the Standard storage class mounted on every instance.",
        "B": "Amazon FSx for Windows File Server with a Multi-AZ deployment joined to the Active Directory domain.",
        "C": "An Amazon EBS io2 Block Express volume with Multi-Attach enabled and attached to all of the application instances.",
        "D": "Amazon FSx for Lustre with a persistent deployment linked to an Amazon S3 bucket."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "B"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option B is Correct\n\n* **Native SMB and NTFS:** FSx for Windows File Server provides fully managed Windows file shares with SMB access and NTFS ACLs.\n* **Active Directory integration:** The file system joins AWS Managed Microsoft AD or a self-managed AD, so existing users and groups control access.\n* **Multi-AZ:** A Multi-AZ deployment keeps a standby file server in another AZ and fails over automatically.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option A is incorrect:** Amazon EFS uses the NFS protocol and is intended for Linux workloads; it does not provide SMB shares for Windows.\n* **Option C is incorrect:** EBS Multi-Attach volumes are limited to a single AZ, require a cluster-aware file system, and do not provide SMB or AD-based access control.\n* **Option D is incorrect:** FSx for Lustre is a high-performance file system for Linux compute workloads, not an SMB file share.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **Storage by protocol:** SMB / Windows / AD = FSx for Windows File Server; NFS / Linux = EFS; HPC = FSx for Lustre; multiprotocol / NetApp = FSx for NetApp ONTAP.\n2. **Storage types:** Block = EBS (one AZ); file = EFS/FSx (shared); object = S3 (HTTP API)."
    },
    {
      "domain": 2,
      "task": "2.1",
      "topics": [
        "Amazon DynamoDB",
        "DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX)",
        "Caching strategies"
      ],
      "prompt": "A gaming company stores leaderboard and player data in Amazon DynamoDB. The workload is extremely read-heavy, with many repeated reads of the same items, and the company now needs response times in microseconds instead of single-digit milliseconds. The developers want to make as few application code changes as possible and do not want to write cache invalidation logic. Which solution meets these requirements?",
      "options": {
        "A": "Increase the table's provisioned read capacity and switch to strongly consistent reads.",
        "B": "Create global secondary indexes for the most frequently read attributes.",
        "C": "Add an Amazon ElastiCache cluster and modify the application code to implement lazy loading with a TTL for every cached item.",
        "D": "Deploy a DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX) cluster and point the application's DynamoDB client to the DAX cluster endpoint."
      },
      "isMulti": false,
      "requiredCount": 1,
      "correctAnswers": [
        "D"
      ],
      "explanation": "### ✅ Why Option D is Correct\n\n* **Microsecond reads:** DAX is an in-memory, write-through cache purpose-built for DynamoDB that delivers microsecond read latency for cached items.\n* **API compatible:** DAX is compatible with DynamoDB API calls, so the application uses the DAX client with the same operations and needs minimal code changes.\n* **Managed cache behavior:** DAX handles item and query caching and writes through to DynamoDB, so developers do not write invalidation logic.\n\n---\n\n### ❌ Why Other Options Are Incorrect\n\n* **Option A is incorrect:** More capacity does not change DynamoDB's single-digit-millisecond latency, and strongly consistent reads do not reduce latency.\n* **Option B is incorrect:** Indexes support different access patterns but do not provide microsecond latency.\n* **Option C is incorrect:** ElastiCache can provide low latency but requires the developers to write and maintain caching and invalidation logic in the application.\n\n---\n\n### 💡 SAA-C03 Exam Concept & AWS Best Practice\n\n1. **\"DynamoDB + microseconds + minimal code change\":** DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX).\n2. **Caching strategies:** Lazy loading = cache on read miss (can be stale); write-through = update cache on write (fresher, more writes)."
    },
